\begin{remark}\label{remark:cheapvsexpensive}
Error estimates for the corrected Krylov approximation or improved error estimates such as the improved Hermite quadrature~\eqref{eq:improvedHermiteQuad}
require additional matrix-vector multiplications.
Instead of investing computational effort in improving the error estimate,
one may as well increase the dimension of the standard Krylov subspace.
For comparison we test the original Expokit code, the corrected Krylov approximation with error estimate $\Era^+$
and the improved Hermite quadrature~\eqref{eq:improvedHermiteQuad} with Krylov subspace $m-1$.
Table~\ref{Table:ExpensivErrEstvsCheapErrEst} shows that a standard Krylov approximation with dimension $m$ leads to better results,
although all considered versions use the same number
of matrix-vector multiplications.
Since the reliability of error estimates such as $\Era$
has been demonstrated earlier,
it appears that additional cost to improve the error estimate
is not justified.

